[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it holds three
problems, and the queued parser fix closes only the first. Each now has a ticket of its own.

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser fix will not stop the other two. Keep #4471 open until all three tickets are closed.
</Callout>

## The three problems

**`parser`: day and month swap.** The shared parser reads every date month first, and every
tenant on file writes the day first. "4 March turned into 3 April." Fix: parse in the tenant's
locale.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form asks for the wrong order.** Its date field prints `MM/DD/YYYY`
but reads the date day first. People type what the hint says, and the booking swaps. Only form
reports from tenants on booking-v3. The parser fix does not touch it. Fix: print the hint in the
order the form reads.

**`move`: one day early, no swap.** "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday.
Same meeting." The date is right and lands a day earlier: a time zone conversion, not parsing.
The desk already read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`); the #4471 table still files
them under `parser` by tenant.

## How they were told apart

- Counted by report id: the export repeats some rows across its pages.
- Cause from the tenant (where they are, which form they run) and the report itself, not from
  the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser and is wrong for many of these.
- Every report body was read. Only the one-day moves describe something other than a swap.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone, and the
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) says "when unsure, merge". Three bugs share the same subjects
("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so all of them landed in one ticket. Merging on
cause, as the triage explorer already checks, would have kept them apart. That needs a new
decision record to replace 0001.

